    {
      "scenario_id": "KA-05-setting",
      "run_key": "master-tot-critic-on-run-2/3/KA-05-setting",
      "expected_faults": [
        "wrong_setting"
      ],
      "final_diagnosis": "The failures are not explained by a CNC-05-specific fault. Jobs 1 and 2 ran on CNC-05 and failed, job 3 ran on CNC-04 and passed, and job 4 ran on CNC-03 and failed, so a cause limited to CNC-05 cannot account for the job-4 failure on CNC-03 (E3, E18). Machine-specific checks rule out the main CNC-05 failure modes: CNC-05 hydraulic pressure is very steady (~68–71, no instability across failed-job vs passed-job windows, E20); CNC-05 following error is not higher than CNC-01's (CNC-01 means are comparable or higher, e.g. 4.995 vs 3.746 in ticks 360–389, E15 vs E17); CNC-05 vibration is stable (E19); and CNC-05 speed is essentially standard at 902 rpm (E6). The pattern is most consistent with a common or line-level cause—incoming material lot variation, inspection-station inconsistency, or shared tooling—rather than one machine. Confidence is moderate (~0.55), below the 0.90 needed to authorize an intervention, and none of the approved machine actions (bearing replacement, speed reset, servo drive, hydraulics repair) would fix a material/inspection common cause. Evidence that would raise confidence: material certificates/receiving inspection for the lots used in jobs 1, 2 and 4 versus job 3, an inspection-station repeatability check, and dimensional checks on rejected parts to see whether job 4 shares the same material lot as jobs 1 and 2.",
